WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:07.259
To start off this course, we'll look at a set of Twinmotion tools that are designed to make your work easier.

2
00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:13.419
Many of those are the types of tools that you would normally spend time building in real-time engines using code,

3
00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:16.489
but here, they are ready to be used right out of the box.

4
00:00:16.489 --> 00:00:20.489
To learn about these tools, you need to fire up Twinmotion.

5
00:00:20.489 --> 00:00:28.649
I am using Community version 2023.2, which was the latest release version at the time I was recording these videos.

6
00:00:28.649 --> 00:00:33.649
You also need to open the project named One Tools that was provided to you.

7
00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:37.649
This project shows the end results of the part 1 portion of this class.

8
00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:42.649
First, we'll look at animators. Zoom in on the doors on the main building's second level.

9
00:00:42.649 --> 00:00:46.649
You can switch camera speeds using the 1 through 6 hotkeys.

10
00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:51.000
The idea is to make these doors open as the camera approaches.

11
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:58.000
In Unreal Engine, you could write a blueprint to that effect if you know how to code, but here, it is quite a bit easier.

12
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.380
Before you start though, look at the scene graph.

13
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:07.450
You'll see it's well organized through the use of containers, making things easily sorted and accessible.

14
00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:12.450
Let's stick with that principle and create a new container to store the door animation.

15
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:18.450
Collapse containers to see better, and then right-click the main scene graph entry and create a new container.

16
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.450
Name it Tools.

17
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.439
With a right-click, make that container active.

18
00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:28.439
Anything you add to the scene now will be part of that container.

19
00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:32.439
Go to the Assets library under Tools, Animators.

20
00:01:32.439 --> 00:01:36.599
Here, you can choose between our Rotation and Translation tools.

21
00:01:36.599 --> 00:01:41.599
Since we're dealing with pivot instead of sliding doors, we'll use rotators.

22
00:01:41.599 --> 00:01:45.890
Here you will find the rotator tool that you need as well as a few samples.

23
00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:50.980
Simply drag the rotator tool and place it in front of the doors in the scene.

24
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.590
Reposition it to where the hinges of the door should be.

25
00:01:54.590 --> 00:02:00.459
If you accidentally click somewhere else, the rotator disappears from the view.

26
00:02:00.459 --> 00:02:04.459
To select it again, you need to do so in the scene graph.

27
00:02:04.459 --> 00:02:09.840
To make the door react to the rotator, you need to make it a child of the tool.

28
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.969
Select the door in the viewport.

29
00:02:11.969 --> 00:02:19.969
Normally, you'd need to drag it from one container to the next in the scene graph, but that's sometimes difficult if there are too many objects listed.

30
00:02:19.969 --> 00:02:24.099
What I find to be a good alternative is to use Cut and Paste.

31
00:02:24.099 --> 00:02:33.159
So with the door selected, press Ctrl-X to cut it to memory, and then press Ctrl-V to paste it back again.

32
00:02:33.159 --> 00:02:39.159
Visually, nothing happens in the viewport, but in the scene graph, the door has now moved to the active container.

33
00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:44.610
All that's left to do is hierarchically link it to the rotator with a click and drag.

34
00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.830
The effect is immediate and continuous.

35
00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:51.060
Select the rotator helper in the scene graph.

36
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.060
There are different animation modes to choose from.

37
00:02:54.060 --> 00:03:02.430
You can also control speed and delay.

38
00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:22.050
To make the rotator react to the camera position, set the animation mode to Once, and then activate the Trigger option.

39
00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:49.939
You can adjust the trigger distance or zone, set it to about 400cm or 4m, adjust the angle for the door opening, set it to a positive or negative value to set the door to open to the inside or to the outside.

40
00:03:49.939 --> 00:04:01.520
As an exercise, do the same for the other door.

41
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:24.899
Let's look at another tool that Twinmotion has to offer.

42
00:04:24.899 --> 00:04:26.899
Section Cubes.

43
00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:30.449
These are found under Tools, Sections.

44
00:04:30.449 --> 00:04:35.689
Drag the Section Cube into the scene.

45
00:04:35.689 --> 00:04:37.689
Move it around to see the effect.

46
00:04:37.689 --> 00:04:45.220
As the name implies, the Section Cube cuts through the geometry to create cross-sections in your project.

47
00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:48.670
You can use Transform Tools to reshape the cube.

48
00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:59.129
You can also invert the operation if you so choose.

49
00:04:59.129 --> 00:05:17.579
You can change the highlight color.

50
00:05:17.579 --> 00:05:21.670
Go ahead and hide the section box before moving on.

51
00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.670
Another tool you may find interesting is the Notes tool.

52
00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:30.670
You can add visual notes to the screen which you can potentially use as part of your design review.

53
00:05:30.769 --> 00:05:33.899
The process is very simple, yet again.

54
00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:45.720
From the Assets library, go to Tools, Notes, and drag a note into the scene into a particular spot, let's say the cafe's entrance.

55
00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:53.939
You can control its size, rotate it around, or you may opt to having it always facing the camera.

56
00:05:53.939 --> 00:06:04.579
To control the space between the text box and its node, you can select the child object in the scene graph and move it around.

57
00:06:04.579 --> 00:06:22.389
You can change the color, or alignment, and of course write down a given note.

58
00:06:22.389 --> 00:06:28.509
In my case, I'm shamelessly paraphrasing a famous movie quote.

59
00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:33.269
Experiment by placing a few more notes in the scene.

60
00:06:33.269 --> 00:06:38.579
Just make sure you hide them before moving on.

61
00:06:38.579 --> 00:06:47.339
Another simple but effective tool is the Measure tool.

62
00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:50.399
You'll find it under Tools, Measure.

63
00:06:50.399 --> 00:06:56.939
It's pretty self-explanatory, so we'll only be spending a passing moment on it using it inside the main building.

64
00:06:56.939 --> 00:07:01.740
Drag it into the scene and see how it snaps onto surfaces.

65
00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:09.160
With it, you can easily measure the distance between surfaces, such as between two walls or between two floors.

66
00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:33.709
Constraints are on by default and you can make adjustments to the color, text position, and global scale.

67
00:07:33.709 --> 00:07:38.439
The dimension text is made to follow the camera.

68
00:07:38.439 --> 00:07:45.439
If you're planning to use the same color for many different objects, consider storing the color so you can access it more easily.

69
00:07:45.439 --> 00:07:53.990
This is definitely more of a design review tool than a rendering tool but still quite effective.

70
00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:58.339
Before you move on, hide the Tools container.

71
00:07:58.339 --> 00:08:03.529
There's one more tool we need to mention, Reflection Probes.

72
00:08:03.529 --> 00:08:08.529
Reflection Probes are used to improve the quality of reflections in real-time environments.

73
00:08:08.529 --> 00:08:16.529
However, it's worth noting that Reflection Probes are somewhat old technology and may not be so necessary as we move forward.

74
00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:21.779
Let's see how reflections are currently handled in the scene by looking at the façade of the main building.

75
00:08:21.779 --> 00:08:28.199
You can see the tree reflected in the glass panes but it's blurry and unconvincing.

76
00:08:28.199 --> 00:08:35.519
What's worse is that if you start moving closer to the façade, there comes a point where that reflection disappears.

77
00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:40.519
This coincides with the moment where the tree is outside the bounds of the viewport.

78
00:08:40.519 --> 00:08:46.320
Twinmotion uses a technique called Screen Space Reflection or SSR.

79
00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:51.450
This means that for reflection to take place, the reflected object must be seen in the viewport.

80
00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:58.450
As you can see, it's not an optimal solution in AEC, although you can get away with it in games environments.

81
00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:02.450
Instead, you can use Reflection Probes to remedy that.

82
00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:07.700
You'll find them in the Asset Library under Tools, Reflection Probes.

83
00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:14.700
Reflection Probes capture the environment around them and project it back onto nearby reflective surfaces.

84
00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:18.990
There are two types available, Spheres and Boxes.

85
00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:21.990
Drag a sphere reflection probe close to the curtain wall.

86
00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:29.259
Currently it doesn't have much effect, we are still only seeing the screen space reflections.

87
00:09:30.259 --> 00:09:33.259
You need to adjust the area of influence of the sphere.

88
00:09:33.259 --> 00:09:37.899
Set it to about 8 meters or 800 centimeters.

89
00:09:37.899 --> 00:09:41.899
Now you can see an effect. It's not perfect yet, but it's getting there.

90
00:09:41.899 --> 00:09:47.860
The biggest difference with what we had a moment ago is the fact that if you zoom closer,

91
00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:52.860
you can still see the reflection of the tree even though the tree is not visible in the viewport anymore.

92
00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:56.759
The quality of the reflections is adjustable.

93
00:09:56.759 --> 00:09:59.889
Reflection captures are essentially bitmaps.

94
00:09:59.889 --> 00:10:03.919
You can increase or decrease the quality in the Preferences dialog.

95
00:10:03.919 --> 00:10:12.049
This is done under Edit, Preferences, where you can edit probes for resolution both in the viewport and at render time.

96
00:10:12.049 --> 00:10:17.049
I have already set them to 1024, but the default is lower at 256.

97
00:10:17.049 --> 00:10:22.419
Try different values to test viewport reflection quality.

98
00:10:22.419 --> 00:10:32.970
However, I did mention earlier that reflection probes are a bit obsolete by today's standards.

99
00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:36.970
This is because rendering engines have evolved quite a bit in the last few years.

100
00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:41.259
Delete the reflection probe to go back to the SSR display.

101
00:10:41.259 --> 00:10:47.110
In the Ambience panel, switch to Render mode.

102
00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:54.389
Note how this project is set in real-time and uses the standard Global Illumination method.

103
00:10:54.389 --> 00:10:58.320
Switch from Standard to Lumen.

104
00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:03.320
Lumen is the newer rendering technology used in both Point Motion and Unreal Engine.

105
00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:08.830
For all intents and purposes, you should rely on it as your go-to rendering method.

106
00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:14.539
You can see the effect immediately. The reflections are high quality and very accurate.

107
00:11:14.539 --> 00:11:20.629
What's more, the rendering in general is far superior. This can be mostly seen inside the building.

108
00:11:20.629 --> 00:11:26.110
Note the effect of bouncing light and color bleed on the various surfaces.

109
00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:32.750
Switching back and forth will give you a better appreciation of the differences between Lumen and the older standard method.

110
00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:43.659
There is a third option. You could use path tracing, which acts as a traditional renderer giving you the best quality rendering available.

111
00:11:43.659 --> 00:11:53.279
You can adjust the quality by using presets or by editing the rendering parameters.

112
00:11:53.279 --> 00:11:58.789
Keep in mind that you need a high-end graphics card for path tracing to work.
